{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"Timbs v. Indiana\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2019-02-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"586 U.S. 146; No. 17-1091\",\"proposition\":\"The Eighth Amendment Excessive Fines Clause applies to the States and reaches punitive civil in-rem forfeitures, supplying the constitutional proportionality constraint on state asset forfeiture.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"civil asset forfeiture—Excessive Fines incorporation\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/586/17-1091/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Nieves v. Bartlett\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2019-05-28\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"587 U.S. 391; No. 17-1174\",\"proposition\":\"Probable cause ordinarily defeats a retaliatory-arrest claim, subject to a narrow exception supported by objective evidence that officers normally do not arrest for the charged offense; Gonzalez later rejected an unduly comparator-specific reading of that exception.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"retaliatory arrest—probable-cause bar and exception\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/587/17-1174/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Thompson v. Clark\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2022-04-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"596 U.S. 36; No. 20-659\",\"proposition\":\"A Fourth Amendment malicious-prosecution plaintiff satisfies favorable termination by showing that the prosecution ended without a conviction; an affirmative indication of innocence is unnecessary.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"malicious prosecution—favorable termination\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/596/20-659/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Egbert v. Boule\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2022-06-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"596 U.S. 482; No. 21-147\",\"proposition\":\"Courts may not extend Bivens whenever there is any rational reason to think Congress is better positioned to authorize damages; an alternative remedial process need not award damages, and Bivens does not extend to Border Patrol Fourth Amendment or First Amendment retaliation claims.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens—near-closure of implied-remedy expansion\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/596/21-147/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Hicks v. Ferreyra\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2023-03-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"64 F.4th 156; No. 22-1339\",\"proposition\":\"Bivens remains available for unjustified roadside seizures by line-level U.S. Park Police officers because ordinary domestic policing closely resembles Bivens itself; the court upheld the plaintiff's verdict.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—original Fourth Amendment context survives\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.courtlistener.com/?q=%2264%20F.4th%20156%22&type=r\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Xi v. Haugen\",\"court\":\"3d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2023-05-24\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"68 F.4th 824; No. 21-2798\",\"proposition\":\"Egbert foreclosed Bivens claims arising from an FBI national-security investigation, but alleged constitutional violations were not categorically protected by the FTCA discretionary-function exception; the resulting FTCA split became central in Martin.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert and FTCA—investigative misconduct\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.courtlistener.com/?q=%2268%20F.4th%20824%22&type=r\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Snowden v. Henning\",\"court\":\"7th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2023-06-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"72 F.4th 237\",\"proposition\":\"A DEA agent's allegedly excessive force during a routine domestic arrest remained within Bivens's original Fourth Amendment context notwithstanding Egbert, helping create the circuit split later sharpened by Arias, Logsdon, and Robinson.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—domestic-arrest excessive force\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.courtlistener.com/?q=%2272%20F.4th%20237%22&type=r\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Ingram v. Wayne County\",\"court\":\"6th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2023-09-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"81 F.4th 603\",\"proposition\":\"The en banc court required a prompt post-seizure hearing to test the government's continued retention of vehicles pending civil forfeiture; Culley subsequently rejected the federal due-process premise of that separate retention-hearing rule.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"civil asset forfeiture—prompt retention hearing\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.courtlistener.com/?q=%2281%20F.4th%20603%22&type=r\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Logsdon v. U.S. Marshal Service\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-01-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"91 F.4th 1352; No. 22-3238\",\"proposition\":\"Egbert forecloses a new Bivens remedy for alleged excessive force by deputy marshals serving on a USMS fugitive task force; USMS grievance and DOJ Inspector General processes qualify as alternative remedial mechanisms despite offering no damages.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—federal task-force excessive force\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/22-3238/22-3238-2024-01-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Barnes v. Felix\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-01-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"91 F.4th 393; No. 22-20519\",\"proposition\":\"The court applied its moment-of-threat rule, examining only the circumstances immediately surrounding the fatal shots and affirming judgment for the officer; the Supreme Court later rejected that temporal restriction.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"excessive force—superseded moment-of-threat rule\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/22-20519/22-20519-2024-01-23.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Villarreal v. City of Laredo\",\"court\":\"5th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2024-01-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"94 F.4th 374; No. 20-40359\",\"proposition\":\"A divided en banc court granted qualified immunity on First and Fourth Amendment claims arising from a citizen-journalist's arrest for soliciting nonpublic police information; the Supreme Court vacated and remanded for reconsideration under Gonzalez.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"retaliatory arrest—qualified immunity and probable cause\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/20-40359/20-40359-2024-01-23.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Culley v. Marshall\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2024-05-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"601 U.S. 377; No. 22-585\",\"proposition\":\"Due process requires a timely forfeiture hearing after seizure of personal property but does not require a separate preliminary hearing addressing continued retention; the $8,850/Barker timeliness framework, not Mathews, governs.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"civil asset forfeiture—post-seizure process\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/22-585_k5fm.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Mohamed v. Jones\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-05-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"100 F.4th 1214; No. 22-1453\",\"proposition\":\"A district-court order recognizing a Bivens remedy, standing alone and without a preserved immunity issue, is not immediately appealable under the collateral-order doctrine.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens—interlocutory appellate jurisdiction\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/22-1453/22-1453-2024-05-07.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Plowright v. Miami-Dade County\",\"court\":\"11th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-06-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"102 F.4th 1358; No. 23-10425\",\"proposition\":\"Killing a domestic animal is a Fourth Amendment seizure, and shooting an incapacitated, nonthreatening dog was unconstitutional with obvious clarity even without factually identical precedent; claims against the county and police chief remained dismissed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"§1983/qualified immunity—property seizure and obvious clarity\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/23-10425/23-10425-2024-06-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Robinson v. Sauls\",\"court\":\"11th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-06-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"102 F.4th 1337; No. 23-10719\",\"proposition\":\"No Bivens remedy exists for Fourth Amendment excessive force by USMS joint-task-force members executing a warrant; the task-force structure presents a new context, and intergovernmental consequences plus USMS and Inspector General remedies counsel hesitation.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—joint task forces\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://media.ca11.uscourts.gov/opinions/pub/files/202310719.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Chiaverini v. City of Napoleon\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2024-06-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"602 U.S. 556; No. 23-50\",\"proposition\":\"Probable cause for one charge does not categorically defeat a Fourth Amendment malicious-prosecution claim based on another baseless charge; the analysis is charge specific, with causation and damages left for remand.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"malicious prosecution—charge-specific probable cause\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/23-50_n648.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Gonzalez v. Trevino\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2024-06-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"602 U.S. 653; No. 22-1025\",\"proposition\":\"Nieves's no-probable-cause exception does not require virtually identical arrested and nonarrested comparators; objective evidence that officers normally do not arrest anyone for the offense may support a retaliatory-arrest claim.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"retaliatory arrest—Nieves exception\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/22-1025_1a72.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Sheikh v. Department of Homeland Security\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-07-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-16983\",\"proposition\":\"Fourth and Fifth Amendment claims that HSI agents fabricated a search-warrant affidavit and misled prosecutors present a new Bivens context; prosecutorial intrusion, immigration and cross-border responsibilities, and alternative remedial structures foreclose an implied damages action.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—fabricated warrant evidence and prosecution\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/22-16983/22-16983-2024-07-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Fields v. Federal Bureau of Prisons\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-07-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"109 F.4th 264; No. 23-6246\",\"proposition\":\"A divided panel permitted a narrow Bivens claim against front-line prison officers alleged personally to have beaten a restrained inmate and deliberately blocked all administrative remedies; Goldey later reversed this extension.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—prison excessive force, later reversed\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-6246/23-6246-2024-07-25.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Gonzalez v. Trevino\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-08-02\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"109 F.4th 853; No. 21-50276\",\"proposition\":\"On Supreme Court remand, the panel returned the case to the district court to determine whether Gonzalez's objective evidence satisfies the broadened Nieves exception.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"retaliatory arrest—application on remand\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/21-50276/21-50276-2024-08-02.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Harris v. Town of Southern Pines\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-08-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1791\",\"proposition\":\"Disputed omissions bearing on probable cause precluded qualified immunity on a Fourth Amendment malicious-prosecution claim, and an arrested and detained plaintiff need not have been convicted to pursue a fabrication-of-evidence claim; Monell judgment for the municipality was affirmed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"malicious prosecution/fabrication; Monell distinction\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-1791/23-1791-2024-08-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Meshal v. Commissioner, Georgia Department of Public Safety\",\"court\":\"11th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-09-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-10128\",\"proposition\":\"Officers lacked arguable reasonable suspicion to prolong a completed traffic stop for 91 minutes merely because the driver appeared on the No Fly List and lacked arguable probable cause to search his truck; denial of qualified immunity was affirmed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"§1983/qualified immunity—prolonged stop and vehicle search\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/23-10128/23-10128-2024-09-16.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Hernandez v. Causey\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-12-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"124 F.4th 325; No. 24-60080\",\"proposition\":\"A shooting claim against an ICE agent assisting local police presents a new Bivens context; §1983 also failed because the complaint did not plausibly allege that the federal agent acted under color of state law, although it plausibly alleged excessive force.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert and §1983—federal/state color of law\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/us-5th-circuit/116811226.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Rivera-Guadalupe v. Pierce\",\"court\":\"3d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-12-24\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"124 F.4th 295; Nos. 21-1246 and 23-1792\",\"proposition\":\"After Chiaverini, the any-crime rule governs false arrest but not malicious prosecution; the officer nevertheless received qualified immunity because charge-specific probable cause was not clearly established when the 2017 prosecution began.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"malicious prosecution—charge-by-charge rule and qualified immunity\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca3/23-1792/23-1792-2024-12-24.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Villarreal v. City of Laredo\",\"court\":\"5th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2025-04-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 20-40359\",\"proposition\":\"After the Gonzalez GVR, the en banc court again granted qualified immunity because the 2017 arrest predated Nieves and Reichle had not clearly established a right against a probable-cause-supported retaliatory arrest; it did not decide whether Gonzalez's exception applied on the merits.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"retaliatory arrest—pre-Nieves qualified immunity\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/20-40359/20-40359-2025-04-08.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Linton v. Zorn\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-04-24\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"135 F.4th 19; No. 22-2954\",\"proposition\":\"The court denied qualified immunity, treating Amnesty America as clearly establishing that a rear wristlock or pain-compliance technique used on a nonviolent, passively resisting protester could constitute excessive force; Zorn later summarily reversed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"qualified immunity/excessive force—lower decision later reversed\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca2/22-2954/22-2954-2025-04-24.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Barnes v. Felix\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2025-05-15\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"605 U.S. 73; No. 23-1239\",\"proposition\":\"Fourth Amendment deadly-force reasonableness depends on the totality of the circumstances, including events preceding the precise moment of threat; courts may not impose the Fifth Circuit's chronological moment-of-threat blinders.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"excessive force—totality of circumstances\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/24pdf/23-1239_onjq.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Martin v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2025-06-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"605 U.S. 395; No. 24-362\",\"proposition\":\"For FTCA wrong-house-raid claims, the law-enforcement proviso overrides only the intentional-tort exception, not the discretionary-function exception; the United States has no free-standing Supremacy Clause defense, and conventional FTCA analysis governs on remand.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"FTCA—civil remedy for federal wrong-house raids\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/24-362\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Goldey v. Fields\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2025-06-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-809\",\"proposition\":\"The Court summarily reversed the Fourth Circuit and refused to create a Bivens remedy for federal-prison excessive force; even unusually egregious allegations and interference with administrative remedies do not authorize a new implied damages action.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—prison excessive force\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/24-809\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Arias v. Herzon\",\"court\":\"1st Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-08-15\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"150 F.4th 27; No. 23-1618\",\"proposition\":\"A Fourth Amendment excessive-force claim against DEA agents making a routine domestic arrest remains within Bivens's original context; later-created Inspector General complaint procedures did not transform it into a new context, sharpening a circuit split.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—original-context survival and circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca1/23-1618/23-1618-2025-08-15.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Enriquez-Perdomo v. Newman\",\"court\":\"6th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-08-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"149 F.4th 623; No. 24-5714\",\"proposition\":\"Even an alleged warrantless, probable-cause-free ICE arrest and eight-day detention presents a new Bivens context because it occurred outside a home during immigration enforcement; INA complaint procedures and habeas remedies foreclose Fourth and Fifth Amendment damages claims.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Bivens/Egbert—immigration arrest and detention\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.opn.ca6.uscourts.gov/opinions.pdf/25a0229p-06.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Olivier v. City of Brandon\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-03-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"607 U.S. ___; No. 24-993\",\"proposition\":\"Heck v. Humphrey does not bar a §1983 action seeking purely prospective declaratory or injunctive relief, even when success would imply that the plaintiff's earlier conviction under the challenged law was invalid.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"§1983—Heck and prospective relief\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-993/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Zorn v. Linton\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-03-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"607 U.S. 568; No. 25-297\",\"proposition\":\"Qualified immunity protected an officer who, after warnings and repeated noncompliance, used a routine rear wristlock to remove a sit-in protester; precedent concerning varied force against protesters did not clearly establish the unlawfulness of this specific use of force.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"qualified immunity—specificity in excessive-force precedent\",\"discovery_path\":\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/607us2r21_2bo2.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"}}
